PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L12U5.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":12,"cefr":"C2","unit":5,"theme":"Rhetoric and Debate","essential_question":"Is winning an argument the same as being right?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1","title":"Not Only the Conclusion but Also the Reasoning: Correlative Conjunctions and Parallelism","objectives":["Students will be able to use correlative conjunctions with precisely parallel elements.","Students will be able to make verbs agree after either… or / neither… nor and use inversion after a fronted not only.","I can write \"Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.\"","I can write \"Neither the coach nor the players were ready.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام أدوات الربط المزدوجة مع عناصر متوازية بدقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من جعل الفعل يتفق بعد either… or / neither… nor واستخدام القلب بعد not only في أول الجملة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.","أستطيع أن أكتب Neither the coach nor the players were ready."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"motion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmoʊʃən/","definition":"a formal proposal that is debated and voted on","arabic":"مقترح للنقاش / قضية المناظرة","example":"The motion was \"This House would ban homework.\"","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W01"},{"word":"contend","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈtɛnd/","definition":"to argue or claim that something is true","arabic":"يحتجّ / يؤكّد","example":"The opposition contends that the plan is too expensive.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W02"},{"word":"substantiate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/səbˈstæntʃiˌeɪt/","definition":"to provide evidence that proves a claim","arabic":"يُثبت بالدليل","example":"She substantiated her claim with official statistics.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W03"},{"word":"cogent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkoʊdʒənt/","definition":"clear, logical and convincing","arabic":"مقنع ومحكم","example":"He presented a cogent argument for reform.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Correlative conjunctions come in pairs: both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as. The words after each part must be parallel (the same grammatical form): judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue. ✘ (not: judged not on loudness but how cogently they argue).\nPlacement: put each part directly before the parallel elements. ✔ both on serious questions and on light topics ✔ on both serious and light topics ✘ (not: both on serious questions and light topics). ✘ (not: She not only writes speeches but also poems.) → She writes not only speeches but also poems. Agreement: with either… or and neither… nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject: Neither the coach nor the players were ready. Neither the players nor the coach was ready.\nWhen not only begins a sentence, use inversion: Not only did she win the debate, but she also broke the record. rather than also needs parallel forms: Critics say debate rewards cleverness rather than truth. We should listen rather than shout. Whether… or: Whether you agree or disagree, listen carefully.\nboth… and • either… or • neither… nor • not only… but also • whether… or • not… but\neach part directly before parallel elements (same grammatical form)\neither / neither… or / nor + verb agrees with the nearer subject\nNot only + auxiliary + subject… but… also • rather than + parallel form","examples":["Not only the conclusion but also the reasoning was judged.","The Union hosts debates both on serious questions and on light topics.","Speakers must either substantiate their claims or lose points.","They are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.","Neither the coach nor the players were ready.","Not only did she win the debate, but she also broke the record.","Debate should reward truth rather than cleverness.","Whether you agree or disagree, listen carefully."],"common_errors":["✗ She not only writes speeches but also poems. → ✓ She writes not only speeches but also poems. (Parallel placement.)","✗ Neither the players nor the coach were ready. → ✓ Neither the players nor the coach was ready. (Verb agrees with the nearer subject.)","✗ Not only she won the debate, but she also broke the record. → ✓ Not only did she win the debate, but she also broke the record. (Inversion after Not only.)","✗ Speakers must either substantiate their claims or losing points. → ✓ Speakers must either substantiate their claims or lose points. (Parallel verbs.)"],"arabic":"أدوات الربط المزدوجة تأتي أزواجًا: both… and وeither… or وneither… nor وnot only… but also وwhether… or، ويجب أن يكون ما بعد كل جزء متوازيًا في البنية.\nنضع كل جزء مباشرة قبل العنصر المتوازي: She writes not only speeches but also poems.\nمع either… or وneither… nor يتفق الفعل مع الفاعل الأقرب: Neither the coach nor the players were ready.\nإذا بدأت الجملة بـ Not only نقلب: Not only did she win…، وrather than تحتاج أيضًا إلى صيغ متوازية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Not Only the Conclusion but Also the Reasoning: Correlative Conjunctions and Parallelism”. Let’s learn correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U5-LS1 | Not Only the Conclusion but Also the Reasoning: Correlative Conjunctions and Parallelism","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “The Art of Debate”: Much as we may dislike arguments, debate is one of the oldest tools humans have for discovering the truth. In classical Arabic culture, scholars took part in munazara, formal debates in which not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning was judged. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Much as we may dislike arguments, debate is one of the oldest tools humans have for discovering the truth. In classical Arabic culture, scholars took part in munazara, formal debates in which not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning was judged.","visual":"Image GV-L12-U5-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Correlative conjunctions come in pairs: both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as. The words after each part must be parallel (the same grammatical form): judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue. ✘ (not: judged not on loudness but how cogently they argue). Placement: put each part directly before the parallel elements. ✔ both on serious questions and on light topics ✔ on both serious and light topics ✘ (not: both on serious questions and light topics). ✘ (not: She not only writes speeches but also poems.) → She writes not only speeches but also poems. Agreement: with either… or and neither… nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject: Neither the coach nor the players were ready. Neither the players nor the coach was ready.","on_screen":"both… and • either… or • neither… nor • not only… but also • whether… or • not… but • each part directly before parallel elements (same grammatical form) • either / neither… or / nor + verb agrees with the nearer subject","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Not only the conclusion but also the reasoning was judged. The Union hosts debates both on serious questions and on light topics. Speakers must either substantiate their claims or lose points.","on_screen":"Not only the conclusion but also the reasoning was judged. / The Union hosts debates both on serious questions and on light topics. / Speakers must either substantiate their claims or lose points.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “She not only writes speeches but also poems.” That’s not right. We say: “She writes not only speeches but also poems.” Parallel placement.","on_screen":"✗ She not only writes speeches but also poems.   ✓ She writes not only speeches but also poems.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Neither the players nor the coach ___ ready.\" was or were? … Think! … *was* – the nearer subject is *the coach*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","أدوات الربط المزدوجة تأتي أزواجًا: both… and وeither… or وneither… nor وnot only… but also وwhether… or، ويجب أن يكون ما بعد كل جزء متوازيًا في البنية.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: She not only writes speeches but also poems. — الصحيح: She writes not only speeches but also poems.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Art of Debate","body":"Much as we may dislike arguments, debate is one of the oldest tools humans have for discovering the truth. In classical Arabic culture, scholars took part in munazara, formal debates in which not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning was judged. In Britain, the Oxford Union, founded in 1823, has hosted debates both on serious political questions and on light-hearted topics for two centuries.\nA competitive debate has a simple structure. One team proposes a motion; the other opposes it. Speakers must either substantiate their claims with evidence or risk losing points. They are judged neither on how loudly they speak nor on how many facts they can list, but on how cogently they argue.\nHard as it is to defend a position you disagree with, debaters often have no choice, since sides are usually assigned at random. For all its difficulty, this practice teaches a valuable skill: however strongly you believe something, you must understand the best arguments against it.\nCritics contend that competitive debate rewards cleverness rather than truth. Though this criticism has some merit, supporters reply that good debating requires not only speaking persuasively but also listening carefully. Whether students become lawyers, scientists or teachers, the habits of debate (organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly) are likely to serve them well.\nIn an age of angry online arguments, the debating hall offers a different model: disagreement without hostility, and persuasion rather than insults. Albeit imperfect, it remains one of the best schools of reasoned discussion we have.","word_count":255,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"In munazara, not only the conclusion ___ the quality of the reasoning was judged.","options":["and also","but","but also","as well"],"answer":"but also","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"Speakers must either substantiate their claims ___ points.","options":["or lose","or to lose","nor lose","or losing"],"answer":"or lose","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"Neither the players nor the coach ___ ready for the final round.","options":["were","are","have been","was"],"answer":"was","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"___ she win the debate, but she also broke the school record.","options":["Only not did","Not only did","Not only","Not only she"],"answer":"Not only did","explanation":"Not only… but also (with inversion).","explanation_ar":"Not only."},{"q":"","options":["She not only writes speeches but also poems.","She writes not only speeches but also writes poems.","She writes not only speeches but also poems.","She not only writes speeches but poems also."],"answer":"She writes not only speeches but also poems.","explanation":"Placement of correlative parts.","explanation_ar":"موضع أداة الربط."},{"q":"The adjudicators explained that debaters would be judged ______ on the clarity of their reasoning, not on the number of statistics they could quote.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["either","both","not only","primarily"],"answer":"primarily","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct form. Word bank: cleverness, decide, disagree","items":[{"q":"Either the moderator or the adjudicators ______ (decide) who speaks first.","options":[],"answer":"decide","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"Debate should reward truth rather than ______ (clever).","options":[],"answer":"cleverness","explanation":"rather than / whether… or with parallel forms.","explanation_ar":"rather than."},{"q":"Whether you agree or ______ (disagreement), listen carefully.","options":[],"answer":"disagree","explanation":"rather than / whether… or with parallel forms.","explanation_ar":"rather than."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The team practiced both in the morning and the evening.","options":[],"answer":"The team practiced both in the morning and in the evening.","explanation":"both in the morning and the evening → both in the morning and in the evening. Placement of correlative parts.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: both in the morning and the evening → both in the morning and in the evening. موضع أداة الربط."},{"q":"Not only she prepared her own speech, but she also helped her teammates.","options":[],"answer":"Not only did she prepare her own speech, but she also helped her teammates.","explanation":"Not only she prepared → Not only did she prepare. Not only… but also (with inversion).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Not only she prepared → Not only did she prepare. Not only."},{"q":"The judges were impressed not by his confidence but how he answered questions.","options":[],"answer":"The judges were impressed not by his confidence but by how he answered questions.","explanation":"how he → by how he. Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: how he → by how he. التوازي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write balanced sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"both… and…\" about a good debater.","options":[],"answer":"A good debater is both well informed and respectful.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"neither… nor…\".","options":[],"answer":"Neither shouting nor interrupting will help you win a debate.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"Not only did…\".","options":[],"answer":"Not only did our team win the semi-final, but it also received the best-speaker award.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"not… but…\".","options":[],"answer":"Success in debate depends not on talent but on preparation.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"rather than\".","options":[],"answer":"We should listen to understand rather than to reply.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A debate team profile.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences describing your ideal debate partner with correlatives.","options":[],"answer":"My ideal partner would be both calm and quick-thinking. She would either answer every question or admit what she didn't know.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"whether… or…\".","options":[],"answer":"Whether we win or lose, we will learn something valuable.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"either… or…\" where the verb agrees with the nearer subject.","options":[],"answer":"Either the captain or the other speakers are going to present the rebuttal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Hot Seat","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team sits in the hot seat facing the class.","The teacher shows a cue card behind the student; the team gives a clue using correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms without saying the word.","The student in the hot seat guesses the cue word within 30 seconds.","Teams score 1 point for a correct guess and 1 point for a correct clue sentence. Rotate seats."],"data":[{"label":"not only… but also"},{"label":"both on… and on…"},{"label":"either substantiate… or risk…"},{"label":"neither… nor… but"},{"label":"Much as we may dislike"},{"label":"Hard as it is"},{"label":"For all its difficulty"},{"label":"albeit"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The Union hosts debates both on serious questions ___ light-hearted topics.","options":["or on","and on","but on","and"],"answer":"and on","explanation":"Placement of correlative parts.","explanation_ar":"موضع أداة الربط."},{"q":"Neither the coach nor the students ___ expected to win.","options":["were","is","was","has"],"answer":"were","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"Not only ___ well, but she also listened carefully.","options":["did she speak","she did speak","she spoke","spoke she"],"answer":"did she speak","explanation":"Not only… but also (with inversion).","explanation_ar":"Not only."},{"q":"Good debaters persuade with evidence rather than ___.","options":["insult","insulting","to insult","with insults"],"answer":"with insults","explanation":"rather than / whether… or with parallel forms.","explanation_ar":"rather than."},{"q":"Either the first speaker or the second ___ the summary.","options":["are giving","give","have given","gives"],"answer":"gives","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"","options":["The motion was not criticized because it was difficult but because unfair.","The motion was criticized not because difficult but because it was unfair.","The motion was criticized not because it was difficult but because it was unfair.","The motion was criticized not because it was difficult but unfair."],"answer":"The motion was criticized not because it was difficult but because it was unfair.","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"","options":["The training improved both their confidence and they reasoned better.","The training improved both confidence and their reasoning skills improved.","The training both improved their confidence and their reasoning.","The training improved both their confidence and their reasoning."],"answer":"The training improved both their confidence and their reasoning.","explanation":"Placement of correlative parts.","explanation_ar":"موضع أداة الربط."},{"q":"According to the rules, either the team captain or the other team members ______ responsible for answering questions from the audience.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["was","are","is","has been"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"The new coach hoped that the program would teach students not only how to speak confidently ______ to listen respectfully to their opponents.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["and also how","but also how","but how also","but also"],"answer":"but also how","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"Which sentence is correctly parallel?","options":["She is not only intelligent but also she works hard.","She not only is intelligent but also hardworking.","She is not only intelligent but also hardworking.","She is not only intelligent but also works hard."],"answer":"She is not only intelligent but also hardworking.","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A debate championship: two teams at podiums under a banner \"This House Believes…\"; a panel of three adjudicators taking notes; a timer showing 7:00; an audience in a historic hall with wooden benches. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A debate championship: two teams at podiums under a banner \"This House Believes…\"; a panel of three adjudicators taking notes; a timer showing 7:00; an audience in a historic hall with wooden benches"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows motion (a formal proposal that is debated and voted on); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing motion: a formal proposal that is debated and voted on"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows contend (to argue or claim that something is true); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing contend: to argue or claim that something is true"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows substantiate (to provide evidence that proves a claim); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing substantiate: to provide evidence that proves a claim"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows cogent (clear, logical and convincing); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing cogent: clear, logical and convincing"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Hot Seat”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Hot Seat"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W01","script":"motion. motion. The motion was \"This House would ban homework.\"","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W02","script":"contend. contend. The opposition contends that the plan is too expensive.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W03","script":"substantiate. substantiate. She substantiated her claim with official statistics.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-W04","script":"cogent. cogent. He presented a cogent argument for reform.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-T1","script":"Much as we may dislike arguments, debate is one of the oldest tools humans have for discovering the truth. In classical Arabic culture, scholars took part in munazara, formal debates in which not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning was judged. In Britain, the Oxford Union, founded in 1823, has hosted debates both on serious political questions and on light-hearted topics for two centuries. A competitive debate has a simple structure. One team proposes a motion; the other opposes it. Speakers must either substantiate their claims with evidence or risk losing points. They are judged neither on how loudly they speak nor on how many facts they can list, but on how cogently they argue. Hard as it is to defend a position you disagree with, debaters often have no choice, since sides are usually assigned at random. For all its difficulty, this practice teaches a valuable skill: however strongly you believe something, you must understand the best arguments against it. Critics contend that competitive debate rewards cleverness rather than truth. Though this criticism has some merit, supporters reply that good debating requires not only speaking persuasively but also listening carefully. Whether students become lawyers, scientists or teachers, the habits of debate (organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly) are likely to serve them well. In an age of angry online arguments, the debating hall offers a different model: disagreement without hostility, and persuasion rather than insults. Albeit imperfect, it remains one of the best schools of reasoned discussion we have.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Not Only the Conclusion but Also the Reasoning: Correlative Conjunctions and Parallelism”. Let’s learn correlative conjunctions (both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as, rather than); placing each part directly before the parallel elements (both on X and on Y / on both X and Y); agreement with either… or / neither… nor (the verb agrees with the nearer subject); not only… but also with inversion at the start of a sentence; rather than + parallel forms together. Listen to the start of “The Art of Debate”: Much as we may dislike arguments, debate is one of the oldest tools humans have for discovering the truth. In classical Arabic culture, scholars took part in munazara, formal debates in which not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning was judged. Look at the green words. Correlative conjunctions come in pairs: both… and, either… or, neither… nor, not only… but also, whether… or, not… but, as… as. The words after each part must be parallel (the same grammatical form): judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue. ✘ (not: judged not on loudness but how cogently they argue). Placement: put each part directly before the parallel elements. ✔ both on serious questions and on light topics ✔ on both serious and light topics ✘ (not: both on serious questions and light topics). ✘ (not: She not only writes speeches but also poems.) → She writes not only speeches but also poems. Agreement: with either… or and neither… nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject: Neither the coach nor the players were ready. Neither the players nor the coach was ready. Here are some examples. Not only the conclusion but also the reasoning was judged. The Union hosts debates both on serious questions and on light topics. Speakers must either substantiate their claims or lose points. Be careful! Some learners say: “She not only writes speeches but also poems.” That’s not right. We say: “She writes not only speeches but also poems.” Parallel placement. Quick check: \"Neither the players nor the coach ___ ready.\" was or were? … Think! … *was* – the nearer subject is *the coach*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Speakers are judged not on how loudly they speak but on how cogently they argue.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2","title":"Much As I Admire Her, Hard As It Is: Advanced Concessive Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to express concession with sophisticated structures such as much as, hard as it is and for all.","Students will be able to use albeit, notwithstanding and despite with the correct grammar.","I can write \"Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.\"","I can write \"The plan was successful, albeit expensive.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعبير عن التنازل بتراكيب رفيعة مثل much as وhard as it is وfor all.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام albeit وnotwithstanding وdespite بالقواعد الصحيحة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.","أستطيع أن أكتب The plan was successful, albeit expensive."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"undermine","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈʌndɚˌmaɪn/","definition":"to gradually weaken something","arabic":"يقوّض / يُضعف","example":"One false statistic can undermine an entire argument.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W01"},{"word":"bolster","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈboʊlstɚ/","definition":"to support or strengthen something","arabic":"يدعم / يعزّز","example":"New evidence bolstered their case.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W02"},{"word":"articulate","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ɑrˈtɪkjəˌleɪt/","definition":"able to express ideas clearly in words","arabic":"فصيح / واضح التعبير","example":"She is an articulate speaker.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W03"},{"word":"impartial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪmˈpɑrʃəl/","definition":"not supporting either side; fair","arabic":"محايد / نزيه","example":"The judges must be impartial.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Concession means admitting a fact that seems to work against your main point. Beyond although and even though, C2 writers use: Much as + clause (= although… very much): Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb: Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, … Strange though it may seem, … Quickly as he spoke, he made no mistakes.\nhowever + adjective / adverb + subject + verb: However strongly you believe something, consider the other side. Try as + subject + might (= although they tried hard): Try as I might, I couldn't convince them. For all + noun (= despite): For all its faults, debate teaches valuable skills. Albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (formal = although it was): The plan was successful, albeit expensive.\nnotwithstanding (very formal) can come before or after a noun: Notwithstanding the criticism, the motion passed. / The criticism notwithstanding, the motion passed. despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that + clause: Despite losing, they were proud. (not: Despite they lost) ✘. even though = a real fact (Even though it rained, we went.); even if = a condition (Even if it rains, we'll go.).\nMuch as + clause • Adjective + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…)\nHowever + adjective + subject + verb • Try as I might, …\nFor all + noun • albeit + adjective / phrase • notwithstanding + noun\ndespite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that • even though (fact) ≠ even if (condition)","examples":["Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument.","Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.","Strange though it may seem, losing taught us more than winning.","However strongly you believe something, consider the other side.","Try as I might, I couldn't convince the adjudicators.","For all its faults, debate teaches valuable skills.","The plan was successful, albeit expensive.","Despite losing the final, the team was proud of its performance."],"common_errors":["✗ As much as I admire her, but I disagree. → ✓ Much as I admire her, I disagree. (No but after a concessive clause.)","✗ Hard it is as to defend… → ✓ Hard as it is to defend… (Adjective + as + subject + verb.)","✗ Despite they lost, they were proud. → ✓ Despite losing / Despite the fact that they lost, they were proud. (despite + noun / -ing.)","✗ The plan was successful, albeit it was expensive and slow, but useful. → ✓ The plan was successful, albeit expensive. (albeit + adjective / phrase.)"],"arabic":"التنازل يعني الاعتراف بحقيقة تبدو ضد فكرتك ثم متابعة الحجة، ومن تراكيبه الرفيعة: Much as I admire her, …، Hard as it is to…، Strange though it may seem, …\nHowever + صفة + فاعل + فعل، وTry as I might (رغم محاولتي الشديدة)، وFor all + اسم (رغم)، وalbeit + صفة (وإن كان).\nnotwithstanding رسمية جدًّا وتأتي قبل الاسم أو بعده، وdespite + اسم أو -ing أو the fact that، ولا نقول despite they lost.\neven though لحقيقة واقعة، وeven if لشرط محتمل، ولا نضيف but بعد جملة التنازل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Much As I Admire Her, Hard As It Is: Advanced Concessive Clauses”. Let’s learn concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U5-LS2 | Much As I Admire Her, Hard As It Is: Advanced Concessive Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak. Try as they might, they could not find a flaw in her logic. (Concessive clauses are used in statements.)","on_screen":"Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak. | Try as they might, they could not find a flaw in her logic. | (Concessive clauses are used in statements.)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Concession means admitting a fact that seems to work against your main point. Beyond although and even though, C2 writers use: Much as + clause (= although… very much): Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb: Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, … Strange though it may seem, … Quickly as he spoke, he made no mistakes. however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb: However strongly you believe something, consider the other side. Try as + subject + might (= although they tried hard): Try as I might, I couldn't convince them. For all + noun (= despite): For all its faults, debate teaches valuable skills. Albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (formal = although it was): The plan was successful, albeit expensive.","on_screen":"Much as + clause • Adjective + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…) • However + adjective + subject + verb • Try as I might, … • For all + noun • albeit + adjective / phrase • notwithstanding + noun","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice. Strange though it may seem, losing taught us more than winning.","on_screen":"Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. / Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice. / Strange though it may seem, losing taught us more than winning.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “As much as I admire her, but I disagree.” That’s not right. We say: “Much as I admire her, I disagree.” No but after a concessive clause.","on_screen":"✗ As much as I admire her, but I disagree.   ✓ Much as I admire her, I disagree.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Much as I admire her\" = \"Although I ___ admire her\"? … Think! … *very much*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","التنازل يعني الاعتراف بحقيقة تبدو ضد فكرتك ثم متابعة الحجة، ومن تراكيبه الرفيعة: Much as I admire her, …، Hard as it is to…، Strange though it may seem, …","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: As much as I admire her, but I disagree. — الصحيح: Much as I admire her, I disagree.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ I admire her, I disagree with her argument.","options":["As much","Much as","Much as that","Much"],"answer":"Much as","explanation":"Much as + clause.","explanation_ar":"Much as."},{"q":"___ to defend a position you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.","options":["Hard it is as","Hard though is it","As hard it is","Hard as it is"],"answer":"Hard as it is","explanation":"Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"Hard as it is."},{"q":"___, I couldn't convince the adjudicators.","options":["Try as I might","Try as might I","Trying might I","As I might try"],"answer":"Try as I might","explanation":"However + adjective; Try as I might.","explanation_ar":"Try as I might."},{"q":"___ its faults, debate teaches valuable skills.","options":["For all","Although","In spite","Despite of"],"answer":"For all","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"The plan was successful, ___ expensive.","options":["despite","in spite","albeit","although it"],"answer":"albeit","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"______ the strong opposition from several council members, the motion to build a new public library passed by a narrow margin.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Even though","Albeit","Much as","Notwithstanding"],"answer":"Notwithstanding","explanation":"despite / notwithstanding grammar.","explanation_ar":"despite."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with the words in brackets.","items":[{"q":"Although it seems strange, losing taught us more than winning. (Strange though)  (concessive inversion)","options":[],"answer":"Strange though it may seem, losing taught us more than winning.","explanation":"Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"Hard as it is."},{"q":"Although we lost the final, we were proud. (Despite)  (despite + -ing)","options":[],"answer":"Despite losing the final, we were proud.","explanation":"despite / notwithstanding grammar.","explanation_ar":"despite."},{"q":"Although I tried very hard, I couldn't finish my speech in time. (Try as I might)","options":[],"answer":"Try as I might, I couldn't finish my speech in time.","explanation":"However + adjective; Try as I might.","explanation_ar":"Try as I might."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"As much as I respect him, but his evidence is weak.","options":[],"answer":"Much as I respect him, his evidence is weak.","explanation":"As much as I respect him, but → Much as I respect him,. Much as + clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: As much as I respect him, but → Much as I respect him,. Much as."},{"q":"Despite they practiced for weeks, they lost in the first round.","options":[],"answer":"Despite practicing for weeks, they lost in the first round.","explanation":"Despite they practiced → Despite practicing. despite / notwithstanding grammar.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Despite they practiced → Despite practicing. despite."},{"q":"We'll hold the debate outdoors even though it rains. (We don't know yet.)","options":[],"answer":"We'll hold the debate outdoors even if it rains. (We don't know yet.)","explanation":"even though → even if. even though vs even if.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: even though → even if. even though أم even if."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Concede and continue.","items":[{"q":"Begin with \"Much as I…\".","options":[],"answer":"Much as I love social media, I think it makes debate more aggressive.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"Hard / Difficult as it is…\".","options":[],"answer":"Difficult as it is to admit, my opponent had the stronger evidence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Give advice to a nervous debater beginning \"However confident…\".","options":[],"answer":"However confident you feel, always prepare a rebuttal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"For all…\".","options":[],"answer":"For all its benefits, technology cannot replace face-to-face discussion.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Use \"albeit\" after a noun or adjective.","options":[],"answer":"We reached an agreement, albeit a temporary one.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A gracious concession speech.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences congratulating the winning team with concessive structures.","options":[],"answer":"Much as we hoped to win, we must congratulate our opponents. Hard as it is to lose, we learned a great deal today.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"Strange though it may seem…\".","options":[],"answer":"Strange though it may seem, I enjoyed arguing the side I disagreed with.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"notwithstanding\".","options":[],"answer":"The result notwithstanding, we are proud of our performance.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Sentence Builder Race","mechanic":"sentence_builder","rules":["Each team receives the same tile set.","In 5 minutes, build as many different correct sentences as possible using concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if.","Write each sentence on the team sheet.","Swap sheets with another team to check. One point per correct sentence; no points for repeats."],"data":[{"cue":"the conclusion / the reasoning / judge (not only… but also)"},{"cue":"serious topics / light topics (both on… and on…)"},{"cue":"substantiate claims / lose points (either… or)"},{"cue":"loudly / many facts / cogently (neither… nor… but)"},{"cue":"we / dislike arguments / debate / be useful (Much as…)"},{"cue":"it / be hard / defend a view you disagree with (Hard as…)"},{"cue":"its difficulty / the practice / teach skills (For all…)"},{"cue":"the reply / be brief / clear (albeit)"},{"cue":"I / try hard / I / not convince them (Try as I might)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ I enjoy debating, I find it exhausting.","options":["As much","So much as","Much as","Much"],"answer":"Much as","explanation":"Much as + clause.","explanation_ar":"Much as."},{"q":"___ though it may sound, I prefer arguing the opposite side.","options":["Strange","Strangely","Strangeness","Stranger"],"answer":"Strange","explanation":"Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"Hard as it is."},{"q":"___ well she spoke, she could not change the adjudicators' minds.","options":["Although","Whatever","Despite","However"],"answer":"However","explanation":"However + adjective; Try as I might.","explanation_ar":"Try as I might."},{"q":"The speech was effective, ___ a little long.","options":["for all","albeit","despite","although it"],"answer":"albeit","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"___ the fact that she was nervous, she spoke brilliantly.","options":["Although","Despite","Albeit","Even though"],"answer":"Despite","explanation":"despite / notwithstanding grammar.","explanation_ar":"despite."},{"q":"","options":["Even though she was tired, she prepared her rebuttal.","Even she was tired, she prepared her rebuttal.","Even if she was tired, she prepared her rebuttal yesterday.","Even though she is tired tomorrow, she will prepare."],"answer":"Even though she was tired, she prepared her rebuttal.","explanation":"even though vs even if.","explanation_ar":"even though أم even if."},{"q":"","options":["Quick as spoke he, he made no mistakes.","Quickly as he spoke, but he made no mistakes.","Quickly as he spoke, he made no mistakes.","As quickly he spoke, he made no mistakes."],"answer":"Quickly as he spoke, he made no mistakes.","explanation":"Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"Hard as it is."},{"q":"______ the team's research was, its arguments were difficult to follow because the speakers spoke too quickly.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Albeit admirable","Despite admirable","However admirable","Even admirable"],"answer":"However admirable","explanation":"However + adjective; Try as I might.","explanation_ar":"Try as I might."},{"q":"The committee approved the proposal, ______ with several important changes to its budget and timeline.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["in spite","despite","although it","albeit"],"answer":"albeit","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"What does \"For all its faults, debate is valuable\" mean?","options":["All faults make debate valuable.","Debate is valuable for every fault.","Debate has no faults.","Despite its faults, debate is valuable."],"answer":"Despite its faults, debate is valuable.","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A debate practice room: a student frowning at a card that says \"You must argue FOR the motion\" although her notes say \"AGAINST\"; a coach encouraging her; a whiteboard with \"Much as…\", \"Hard as it is…\", \"For all its faults…\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A debate practice room: a student frowning at a card that says \"You must argue FOR the motion\" although her notes say \"AGAINST\"; a coach encouraging her; a whiteboard with \"Much as…\", \"Hard as it is…\", \"For all its faults…\""},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Rhetoric and Debate”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …)"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows undermine (to gradually weaken something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing undermine: to gradually weaken something"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows bolster (to support or strengthen something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing bolster: to support or strengthen something"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows articulate (able to express ideas clearly in words); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing articulate: able to express ideas clearly in words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows impartial (not supporting either side; fair); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing impartial: not supporting either side; fair"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Sentence Builder Race”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Sentence Builder Race"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W01","script":"undermine. undermine. One false statistic can undermine an entire argument.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W02","script":"bolster. bolster. New evidence bolstered their case.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W03","script":"articulate. articulate. She is an articulate speaker.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-W04","script":"impartial. impartial. The judges must be impartial.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Much As I Admire Her, Hard As It Is: Advanced Concessive Clauses”. Let’s learn concessive structures: much as + clause (Much as I admire her, …); adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb (Hard as it is…, Strange though it may seem…); however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb; try as + subject + might (Try as I might, …); for all + noun (= despite: For all its faults, …); albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (= although); notwithstanding (+ noun, or after a noun); despite / in spite of + noun / -ing / the fact that; even though vs even if together. Remember these patterns: Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak. Try as they might, they could not find a flaw in her logic. (Concessive clauses are used in statements.) Concession means admitting a fact that seems to work against your main point. Beyond although and even though, C2 writers use: Much as + clause (= although… very much): Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb: Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, … Strange though it may seem, … Quickly as he spoke, he made no mistakes. however + adjective / adverb + subject + verb: However strongly you believe something, consider the other side. Try as + subject + might (= although they tried hard): Try as I might, I couldn't convince them. For all + noun (= despite): For all its faults, debate teaches valuable skills. Albeit + adjective / adverb / phrase (formal = although it was): The plan was successful, albeit expensive. Here are some examples. Much as I admire her, I disagree with her argument. Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice. Strange though it may seem, losing taught us more than winning. Be careful! Some learners say: “As much as I admire her, but I disagree.” That’s not right. We say: “Much as I admire her, I disagree.” No but after a concessive clause. Quick check: \"Much as I admire her\" = \"Although I ___ admire her\"? … Think! … *very much*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Hard as it is to defend a view you disagree with, debaters often have no choice.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3","title":"Cogent, Tenuous, Substantiate: The Vocabulary of Debate and Argument","objectives":["Students will be able to evaluate arguments with precise debate vocabulary.","Students will be able to use debate word families accurately.","I can describe an argument as cogent, tenuous or sweeping.","I can use \"contend\", \"contention\" and \"contentious\" correctly."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقييم الحجج بمفردات المناظرة الدقيقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام عائلات كلمات المناظرة بدقة.","أستطيع أن أصف الحجة بأنها محكمة أو واهية أو تعميمية.","أستطيع أن أستخدم contend وcontention وcontentious بشكل صحيح."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"adjudicator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈdʒudɪˌkeɪtɚ/","definition":"a person who judges a competition or a dispute","arabic":"حَكَم","example":"The adjudicators announced the winning team.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W01"},{"word":"discredit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈskrɛdət/","definition":"to make people stop trusting someone or something","arabic":"يُفقد المصداقية / يشكّك في","example":"The lawyer tried to discredit the witness.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W02"},{"word":"reiterate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈɪtɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to repeat something to emphasize it","arabic":"يكرّر / يؤكّد مجددًا","example":"Let me reiterate my main point.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W03"},{"word":"tenuous","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈtɛnjəwəs/","definition":"weak and not convincing","arabic":"واهٍ / ضعيف","example":"The link between the two events is tenuous.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W04"},{"word":"nuance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnuɑns/","definition":"a small but important difference in meaning or opinion","arabic":"فارق دقيق","example":"A good debater understands the nuances of an issue.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W05"},{"word":"sweeping","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈswipɪŋ/","definition":"too general, without considering details or exceptions","arabic":"شامل وعام بلا تمييز","example":"\"All teenagers are lazy\" is a sweeping generalization.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W06"},{"word":"preempt","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈpriˌɛmpt/","definition":"to deal with something before it happens","arabic":"يستبق","example":"She preempted the opposition's argument in her opening speech.","image_id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Debate procedure: one side proposes the motion (the proposition), the other opposes it (the opposition); an impartial chair and adjudicators judge; a point of information is a short question from the other side; a rebuttal answers an opponent's argument. Debaters contend (claim), substantiate (prove with evidence), reiterate (repeat for emphasis) and preempt (answer an objection before it is made).\nEvaluating arguments: cogent (clear and convincing) vs tenuous (weak); a sweeping generalization (too general); nuance (a subtle difference). Effects on arguments: evidence can bolster (strengthen) a case; a false fact can undermine (weaken) it; a speaker can discredit (make people distrust) a source. An articulate speaker expresses ideas clearly.\nWord families: argue → argument → argumentative (likes to argue) → arguably (it can be argued); contend → contention (a claim or a disagreement) → contentious (causing disagreement); persuade → persuasion → persuasive → persuasively; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated (not proven); articulate (adj / v) → articulation; credit → credible → credibility → discredit; concede → concession.\nWord families: argue: argument (n), argumentative (adj), arguably (adv) | contend: contention (n), contentious (adj) | persuade: persuasion (n), persuasive (adj), persuasively (adv) | substantiate: substance (n), substantial (adj), unsubstantiated (adj) | credit: credible (adj), credibility (n), discredit (v) | concede: concession (n)\nCollocations/phrases: propose a motion • oppose a motion • a point of information • the floor is open | a cogent argument • a tenuous link • a sweeping generalization • the nuances of an issue | substantiate a claim • bolster a case • undermine an argument • preempt an objection | an impartial adjudicator • an articulate speaker • discredit a source • reiterate a point","examples":["The proposition team proposed the motion \"This House would ban homework.\"","She substantiated her claim with official statistics.","He presented a cogent argument for reform.","The link between the two events is tenuous.","\"All teenagers are lazy\" is a sweeping generalization.","One false statistic can undermine an entire argument.","She preempted the opposition's main objection in her opening speech.","The topic is highly contentious; people feel strongly on both sides."],"common_errors":["✗ Her claim was unsubstantial, so nobody believed it. → ✓ Her claim was unsubstantiated, so nobody believed it. (Not proven → unsubstantiated.)","✗ He is very argumentive. → ✓ He is very argumentative. (Spelling: argument + ative.)","✗ The new evidence undermined their case and made it stronger. → ✓ The new evidence bolstered their case. (Strengthen → bolster.)","✗ The judge must be partial to both teams. → ✓ The judge must be impartial. (Fair → impartial.)"],"arabic":"إجراءات المناظرة: propose a motion (يقترح القضية) وoppose (يعارض) وpoint of information (سؤال اعتراضي قصير) وrebuttal (رد)، والحَكَم impartial (محايد).\nتقييم الحجج: cogent (محكمة) مقابل tenuous (واهية)، وsweeping generalization (تعميم شامل)، وbolster (يدعم) مقابل undermine (يقوّض)، وdiscredit (يُفقد المصداقية).\nعائلات الكلمات: argue → argument → argumentative → arguably، وcontend → contention → contentious، وsubstantiate → substantial → unsubstantiated."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Cogent, Tenuous, Substantiate: The Vocabulary of Debate and Argument”. Let’s learn debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U5-LS3 | Cogent, Tenuous, Substantiate: The Vocabulary of Debate and Argument","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. adjudicator: a person who judges a competition or a dispute. discredit: to make people stop trusting someone or something. reiterate: to repeat something to emphasize it.","on_screen":"adjudicator | discredit | reiterate","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Debate procedure: one side proposes the motion (the proposition), the other opposes it (the opposition); an impartial chair and adjudicators judge; a point of information is a short question from the other side; a rebuttal answers an opponent's argument. Debaters contend (claim), substantiate (prove with evidence), reiterate (repeat for emphasis) and preempt (answer an objection before it is made). Evaluating arguments: cogent (clear and convincing) vs tenuous (weak); a sweeping generalization (too general); nuance (a subtle difference). Effects on arguments: evidence can bolster (strengthen) a case; a false fact can undermine (weaken) it; a speaker can discredit (make people distrust) a source. An articulate speaker expresses ideas clearly.","on_screen":"debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The proposition team proposed the motion \"This House would ban homework.\" She substantiated her claim with official statistics. He presented a cogent argument for reform.","on_screen":"The proposition team proposed the motion \"This House would ban homework.\" / She substantiated her claim with official statistics. / He presented a cogent argument for reform.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Her claim was unsubstantial, so nobody believed it.” That’s not right. We say: “Her claim was unsubstantiated, so nobody believed it.” Not proven → unsubstantiated.","on_screen":"✗ Her claim was unsubstantial, so nobody believed it.   ✓ Her claim was unsubstantiated, so nobody believed it.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Does \"tenuous\" describe a strong or a weak link? … Think! … Weak. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe an argument as cogent, tenuous or sweeping.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can describe an argument as cogent, tenuous or sweeping.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","إجراءات المناظرة: propose a motion (يقترح القضية) وoppose (يعارض) وpoint of information (سؤال اعتراضي قصير) وrebuttal (رد)، والحَكَم impartial (محايد).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Her claim was unsubstantial, so nobody believed it. — الصحيح: Her claim was unsubstantiated, so nobody believed it.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أصف الحجة بأنها محكمة أو واهية أو تعميمية."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"She ___ her claim with official statistics from the ministry.","options":["reiterated","substantiated","preempted","undermined"],"answer":"substantiated","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"\"All teenagers are lazy\" is a ___ generalization.","options":["sweeping","nuanced","tenuous","cogent"],"answer":"sweeping","explanation":"Debate collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"One false statistic can ___ an entire argument.","options":["substantiate","undermine","bolster","adjudicate"],"answer":"undermine","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"My cousin loves to disagree with everyone; he is very ___.","options":["arguing","argument","arguably","argumentative"],"answer":"argumentative","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The judges must be completely ___ and favor neither team.","options":["impartial","partial","articulate","contentious"],"answer":"impartial","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The opposition argued that the link between video games and poor grades was ______: the study they cited had found only a weak connection in a very small sample.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["sweeping","articulate","tenuous","cogent"],"answer":"tenuous","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"motion","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"a formal proposal that is debated","explanation":"motion → a formal proposal that is debated. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"preempt","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"to answer an objection before it is made","explanation":"preempt → to answer an objection before it is made. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"reiterate","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"to repeat something for emphasis","explanation":"reiterate → to repeat something for emphasis. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"discredit","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"to make people stop trusting someone","explanation":"discredit → to make people stop trusting someone. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"nuance","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"a small but important difference in meaning","explanation":"nuance → a small but important difference in meaning. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"cogent","options":["to repeat something for emphasis","to make people stop trusting someone","a formal proposal that is debated","clear, logical and convincing","to answer an objection before it is made","a small but important difference in meaning"],"answer":"clear, logical and convincing","explanation":"cogent → clear, logical and convincing. Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Evaluate arguments.","items":[{"q":"Give an example of a sweeping generalization and correct it.","options":[],"answer":"\"Social media is bad for everyone\" is sweeping; \"Too much social media can harm some teenagers' sleep\" is more precise.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How can a debater bolster her case?","options":[],"answer":"She can bolster her case with reliable statistics and expert opinions.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Name a contentious topic in your school.","options":[],"answer":"Whether phones should be allowed in class is a contentious topic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Make a bold but careful claim about an art form using \"arguably\".","options":[],"answer":"Arabic calligraphy is arguably the most important art form in Islamic culture.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How can you preempt an objection in a speech?","options":[],"answer":"I can say, \"Some may argue that… however…\" before my opponent raises the point.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: An adjudicator's feedback.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences of feedback for a debater using 3 debate words.","options":[],"answer":"Your argument was cogent and well organized. However, your link between homework and stress was tenuous. Next time, substantiate your claims with data.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences using two forms from the \"contend\" family.","options":[],"answer":"The opposition contends that the plan is too expensive. This contention is supported by the budget figures.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"nuance\".","options":[],"answer":"A strong debater recognizes the nuances of a complex issue.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Family Tree","mechanic":"drag_and_drop","rules":["Teams take turns drawing a word card.","Place the card on the correct branch of the tree and say a sentence with it.","Correct placement and sentence = 2 points.","The team with the most points when the tree is full wins."],"data":[{"word":"argument","category":"noun","family":"argue"},{"word":"argumentative","category":"adjective","family":"argue"},{"word":"arguably","category":"adverb","family":"argue"},{"word":"contention","category":"noun","family":"contend"},{"word":"contentious","category":"adjective","family":"contend"},{"word":"persuasion","category":"noun","family":"persuade"},{"word":"persuasive","category":"adjective","family":"persuade"},{"word":"persuasively","category":"adverb","family":"persuade"},{"word":"substance","category":"noun","family":"substantiate"},{"word":"substantial","category":"adjective","family":"substantiate"},{"word":"unsubstantiated","category":"adjective","family":"substantiate"},{"word":"credible","category":"adjective","family":"credit"},{"word":"credibility","category":"noun","family":"credit"},{"word":"discredit","category":"verb","family":"credit"},{"word":"concession","category":"noun","family":"concede"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The proposition team ___ the motion that homework should be banned.","options":["discredited","proposed","adjudicated","opposed to"],"answer":"proposed","explanation":"Debate collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"New evidence ___ their case and convinced the judges.","options":["preempted","undermined","bolstered","discredited"],"answer":"bolstered","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The lawyer tried to ___ the witness by showing that she had lied before.","options":["bolster","reiterate","substantiate","discredit"],"answer":"discredit","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Let me ___ my main point: education must be accessible to everyone.","options":["preempt","reiterate","undermine","adjudicate"],"answer":"reiterate","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The claim remained ___; no one could find any evidence for it.","options":["unsubstantiated","substantiating","substance","substantial"],"answer":"unsubstantiated","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"She is a highly ___ speaker who expresses complex ideas clearly.","options":["articulate","articulated","articulation","articulately"],"answer":"articulate","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The motion was ___, and the audience was sharply divided.","options":["contend","contending","contention","contentious"],"answer":"contentious","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The ___ announced that the opposition had won by a narrow margin.","options":["nuances","motions","adjudicators","contentions"],"answer":"adjudicators","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Rather than waiting for her opponents to raise the issue of cost, the first speaker ______ the objection by presenting a detailed budget in her opening remarks.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["preempted","discredited","adjudicated","reiterated"],"answer":"preempted","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The adjudicators praised the winning team's ______ case, noting that every claim was supported by evidence and every point followed logically from the last.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["tenuous","contentious","cogent","sweeping"],"answer":"cogent","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"An adjudicators' scoring sheet on a clipboard: categories \"Cogency\", \"Evidence\", \"Rebuttal\", \"Style\"; a word-family tree on the wall: argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; a debater raising a hand for a \"point of information\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An adjudicators' scoring sheet on a clipboard: categories \"Cogency\", \"Evidence\", \"Rebuttal\", \"Style\"; a word-family tree on the wall: argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; a debater raising a hand for a \"point of information\""},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows adjudicator (a person who judges a competition or a dispute); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing adjudicator: a person who judges a competition or a dispute"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows discredit (to make people stop trusting someone or something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing discredit: to make people stop trusting someone or something"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows reiterate (to repeat something to emphasize it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing reiterate: to repeat something to emphasize it"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows tenuous (weak and not convincing); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing tenuous: weak and not convincing"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows nuance (a small but important difference in meaning or opinion); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing nuance: a small but important difference in meaning or opinion"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows sweeping (too general, without considering details or exceptions); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing sweeping: too general, without considering details or exceptions"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows preempt (to deal with something before it happens); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing preempt: to deal with something before it happens"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Family Tree”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Family Tree"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W01","script":"adjudicator. adjudicator. The adjudicators announced the winning team.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W02","script":"discredit. discredit. The lawyer tried to discredit the witness.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W03","script":"reiterate. reiterate. Let me reiterate my main point.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W04","script":"tenuous. tenuous. The link between the two events is tenuous.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W05","script":"nuance. nuance. A good debater understands the nuances of an issue.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W06","script":"sweeping. sweeping. \"All teenagers are lazy\" is a sweeping generalization.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-W07","script":"preempt. preempt. She preempted the opposition's argument in her opening speech.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Cogent, Tenuous, Substantiate: The Vocabulary of Debate and Argument”. Let’s learn debate and argument vocabulary (motion, contend, substantiate, cogent, undermine, bolster, articulate, impartial, adjudicator, discredit, reiterate, tenuous, nuance, sweeping, preempt); debate roles and procedures (the proposition, the opposition, a point of information, a rebuttal, the floor, the chair); collocations (propose / oppose a motion, a cogent argument, a tenuous link, a sweeping generalization, bolster a case, undermine an argument, substantiate a claim, preempt an objection); word families (argue → argument → argumentative → arguably; contend → contention → contentious; persuade → persuasion → persuasive; substantiate → substance → substantial → unsubstantiated) together. Meet some new words. adjudicator: a person who judges a competition or a dispute. discredit: to make people stop trusting someone or something. reiterate: to repeat something to emphasize it. Debate procedure: one side proposes the motion (the proposition), the other opposes it (the opposition); an impartial chair and adjudicators judge; a point of information is a short question from the other side; a rebuttal answers an opponent's argument. Debaters contend (claim), substantiate (prove with evidence), reiterate (repeat for emphasis) and preempt (answer an objection before it is made). Evaluating arguments: cogent (clear and convincing) vs tenuous (weak); a sweeping generalization (too general); nuance (a subtle difference). Effects on arguments: evidence can bolster (strengthen) a case; a false fact can undermine (weaken) it; a speaker can discredit (make people distrust) a source. An articulate speaker expresses ideas clearly. Here are some examples. The proposition team proposed the motion \"This House would ban homework.\" She substantiated her claim with official statistics. He presented a cogent argument for reform. Be careful! Some learners say: “Her claim was unsubstantial, so nobody believed it.” That’s not right. We say: “Her claim was unsubstantiated, so nobody believed it.” Not proven → unsubstantiated. Quick check: Does \"tenuous\" describe a strong or a weak link? … Think! … Weak. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe an argument as cogent, tenuous or sweeping.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4","title":"To Organize, to Anticipate and to Respond: Parallelism in Complex Series","objectives":["Students will be able to keep long series and lists grammatically parallel.","Students will be able to correct faulty parallelism in comparisons and SAT-style sentences.","I can write \"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.\"","I can fix \"Speaking clearly is as important as to listen carefully.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من الحفاظ على التوازي النحوي في السلاسل والقوائم الطويلة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تصحيح أخطاء التوازي في المقارنات والجمل بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أكتب Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.","أستطيع أن أصحح Speaking clearly is as important as to listen carefully."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"motion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmoʊʃən/","definition":"a formal proposal that is debated and voted on","arabic":"مقترح للنقاش / قضية المناظرة","example":"The motion was \"This House would ban homework.\"","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"contend","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈtɛnd/","definition":"to argue or claim that something is true","arabic":"يحتجّ / يؤكّد","example":"The opposition contends that the plan is too expensive.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"substantiate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/səbˈstæntʃiˌeɪt/","definition":"to provide evidence that proves a claim","arabic":"يُثبت بالدليل","example":"She substantiated her claim with official statistics.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cogent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkoʊdʒənt/","definition":"clear, logical and convincing","arabic":"مقنع ومحكم","example":"He presented a cogent argument for reform.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"undermine","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈʌndɚˌmaɪn/","definition":"to gradually weaken something","arabic":"يقوّض / يُضعف","example":"One false statistic can undermine an entire argument.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"bolster","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈboʊlstɚ/","definition":"to support or strengthen something","arabic":"يدعم / يعزّز","example":"New evidence bolstered their case.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"In a series (a list of three or more items), every item must have the same grammatical form. ✔ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. ✔ organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly. ✘ (not: to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response). Long series often hide the error at the end: check the last item against the first.\nFor clarity in long series, you can repeat the article, preposition or to before each item: in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life; to organize…, to anticipate… and to respond… Once you start repeating, continue for every item. Clauses in a series must also match: what speakers say, how they say it and why they say it.\nComparisons must be parallel too: Speaking clearly is as important as listening carefully. (NOT (not: as to listen carefully)) To win is less important than to learn. In bulleted lists, begin every bullet with the same form (all verbs, all nouns or all -ing forms). SAT tip: identify the form of the first item, then choose the option that matches it.\nevery item in a series → the same grammatical form\nrepeat a / the / in / to before each item – or only before the first\ncomparisons: -ing as -ing • to + verb than to + verb\nbulleted lists: all items start the same way","examples":["Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.","Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful.","The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","Speaking clearly is as important as listening carefully.","To win is less important than to learn.","The judges considered what the speakers said, how they said it and why they said it.","Our goals are to research the topic, to divide the arguments and to practice every day.","The coach praised her preparation, her logic and her delivery."],"common_errors":["✗ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response. → ✓ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. (Same form.)","✗ Speaking clearly is as important as to listen carefully. → ✓ Speaking clearly is as important as listening carefully. (Parallel comparison.)","✗ The skills are useful in the classroom, the debating hall and in public life. → ✓ …in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life. (Repeat the preposition consistently.)","✗ The judges considered what the speakers said, how they said it and their reasons. → ✓ …what the speakers said, how they said it and why they said it. (Parallel clauses.)"],"arabic":"في السلسلة يجب أن تكون كل العناصر بالصيغة النحوية نفسها: to organize, to anticipate and to respond، أو organizing, anticipating and responding.\nفي السلاسل الطويلة يمكن تكرار أداة التعريف أو حرف الجر أو to قبل كل عنصر للوضوح، وإذا بدأنا التكرار نستمر فيه.\nالمقارنات أيضًا يجب أن تكون متوازية: Speaking clearly is as important as listening carefully.\nفي القوائم النقطية نبدأ كل نقطة بالصيغة نفسها، ونصيحة SAT: حدّد صيغة العنصر الأول واختر ما يطابقها."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “To Organize, to Anticipate and to Respond: Parallelism in Complex Series”. Let’s learn parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U5-LS4 | To Organize, to Anticipate and to Respond: Parallelism in Complex Series","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"In a series (a list of three or more items), every item must have the same grammatical form. ✔ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. ✔ organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly. ✘ (not: to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response). Long series often hide the error at the end: check the last item against the first.","on_screen":"parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"In a series (a list of three or more items), every item must have the same grammatical form. ✔ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. ✔ organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly. ✘ (not: to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response). Long series often hide the error at the end: check the last item against the first. For clarity in long series, you can repeat the article, preposition or to before each item: in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life; to organize…, to anticipate… and to respond… Once you start repeating, continue for every item. Clauses in a series must also match: what speakers say, how they say it and why they say it.","on_screen":"every item in a series → the same grammatical form • repeat a / the / in / to before each item – or only before the first • comparisons: -ing as -ing • to + verb than to + verb","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful. The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","on_screen":"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. / Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful. / The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Debate teaches students to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response.” That’s not right. We say: “Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.” Same form.","on_screen":"✗ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response.   ✓ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response\" – what's wrong? … Think! … The three items have different forms. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","في السلسلة يجب أن تكون كل العناصر بالصيغة النحوية نفسها: to organize, to anticipate and to respond، أو organizing, anticipating and responding.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Debate teaches students to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response. — الصحيح: Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Debate Club News (editing passage)","body":"This term, our members learned to research motions, to structure arguments and rebutting opposing points. The team competed in Riyadh, in Jeddah and Dammam. The adjudicators praised our clarity, our teamwork and how confident we were. For many members, speaking in public is now as enjoyable as to listen. Next term, we plan to train new members, to host a tournament and we will visit other schools.","word_count":67,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and ______ under pressure.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["a calm response","they respond calmly","responding calmly","to respond calmly"],"answer":"to respond calmly","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"","options":["Good speakers are clarity, confident and respectful.","Good speakers are clear, confidence and respectful.","Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful.","Good speakers are clear, confident and show respect."],"answer":"Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful.","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"Speaking clearly is as important as ___ carefully.","options":["listening","to listen","listen","listened"],"answer":"listening","explanation":"Parallel comparisons.","explanation_ar":"المقارنة المتوازية."},{"q":"","options":["The skills are useful the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","The skills are useful in the classroom, the debating hall and in public life.","The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and public life in."],"answer":"The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life.","explanation":"Repeating articles, prepositions or to.","explanation_ar":"تكرار الأدوات."},{"q":"","options":["The judges considered the speakers' words, how they said it and reasons.","The judges considered what the speakers said, how they said it and their reasons.","The judges considered what the speakers said, the way of saying and why.","The judges considered what the speakers said, how they said it and why they said it."],"answer":"The judges considered what the speakers said, how they said it and why they said it.","explanation":"Parallel clauses.","explanation_ar":"الجمل المتوازية."},{"q":"According to the club's handbook, members are expected to attend weekly meetings, to research each motion carefully and ______ at least two practice debates every month.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["they take part in","to take part in","taking part in","participation in"],"answer":"to take part in","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the faulty parallelism.","items":[{"q":"The coach praised her preparation, her logic and how she delivered.","options":[],"answer":"The coach praised her preparation, her logic and her delivery.","explanation":"how she delivered → her delivery. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: how she delivered → her delivery. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"To win is less important than learning.","options":[],"answer":"To win is less important than to learn.","explanation":"learning → to learn. Parallel comparisons.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: learning → to learn. المقارنة المتوازية."},{"q":"Our goals are to research the topic, to divide the arguments and practicing every day.","options":[],"answer":"Our goals are to research the topic, to divide the arguments and to practice every day.","explanation":"practicing → to practice. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: practicing → to practice. التوازي في السلسلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the faulty parallelism.","items":[{"q":"She enjoys reading, writing and to debate.","options":[],"answer":"She enjoys reading, writing and debating.","explanation":"to debate → debating. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: to debate → debating. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"The motion was discussed in the morning, in the afternoon and the evening.","options":[],"answer":"The motion was discussed in the morning, in the afternoon and in the evening.","explanation":"the evening → in the evening. Repeating articles, prepositions or to.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: the evening → in the evening. تكرار الأدوات."},{"q":"The new rules are fairer, simpler and they are more transparent.","options":[],"answer":"The new rules are fairer, simpler and more transparent.","explanation":"they are more transparent → more transparent. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: they are more transparent → more transparent. التوازي في السلسلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the debate club newsletter.","items":[{"q":"This term, our members learned to research motions, to structure arguments and rebutting opposing points.","options":[],"answer":"This term, our members learned to research motions, to structure arguments and to rebut opposing points.","explanation":"rebutting → to rebut. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: rebutting → to rebut. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"The team competed in Riyadh, in Jeddah and Dammam.","options":[],"answer":"The team competed in Riyadh, in Jeddah and in Dammam.","explanation":"Dammam → in Dammam. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Dammam → in Dammam. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"The adjudicators praised our clarity, our teamwork and how confident we were.","options":[],"answer":"The adjudicators praised our clarity, our teamwork and our confidence.","explanation":"how confident we were → our confidence. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: how confident we were → our confidence. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"For many members, speaking in public is now as enjoyable as to listen.","options":[],"answer":"For many members, speaking in public is now as enjoyable as listening.","explanation":"to listen → listening. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: to listen → listening. التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"Next term, we plan to train new members, to host a tournament and we will visit other schools.","options":[],"answer":"Next term, we plan to train new members, to host a tournament and to visit other schools.","explanation":"we will visit → to visit. Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: we will visit → to visit. التوازي في السلسلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write parallel sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence listing three qualities of a good adjudicator (all adjectives).","options":[],"answer":"A good adjudicator is fair, attentive and consistent.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence listing three things a debater should do (all to-infinitives).","options":[],"answer":"A debater should research the motion, listen to opponents and respond with evidence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a parallel comparison.","options":[],"answer":"Preparing well is more important than speaking fast.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spelling Relay","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["Teams line up facing the board.","The teacher says a word; the first student runs to the board and writes the first letter.","The next student adds the next letter, and so on.","The first team with the correct spelling wins the point."],"data":[{"word":"substantiate","clue":"to provide evidence that proves a claim"},{"word":"cogent","clue":"clear, logical and convincing"},{"word":"impartial","clue":"not supporting either side; fair"},{"word":"adjudicator","clue":"a person who judges a competition or a dispute"},{"word":"tenuous","clue":"weak and not convincing"},{"word":"contentious","clue":""},{"word":"motion","clue":"a formal proposal that is debated and voted on"},{"word":"contend","clue":"to argue or claim that something is true"},{"word":"undermine","clue":"to gradually weaken something"},{"word":"bolster","clue":"to support or strengthen something"},{"word":"articulate","clue":"able to express ideas clearly in words"},{"word":"discredit","clue":"to make people stop trusting someone or something"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The training helped us to think quickly, to speak clearly and to listen carefully.","The training helped us to think quickly, to speak clearly and listening carefully.","The training helped us thinking quickly, to speak clearly and to listen carefully.","The training helped us to think quickly, speaking clearly and to listen carefully."],"answer":"The training helped us to think quickly, to speak clearly and to listen carefully.","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"","options":["Listening to opponents is as valuable as your own speech preparation is.","Listening to opponents is as valuable as to prepare your own speech.","Listening to opponents is as valuable as preparing your own speech.","To listen to opponents is as valuable as preparing your own speech."],"answer":"Listening to opponents is as valuable as preparing your own speech.","explanation":"Parallel comparisons.","explanation_ar":"المقارنة المتوازية."},{"q":"","options":["We practiced at school, home and at the library.","We practiced at school, at home and at the library.","We practiced school, at home and at the library.","We practiced at school, at home and the library."],"answer":"We practiced at school, at home and at the library.","explanation":"Repeating articles, prepositions or to.","explanation_ar":"تكرار الأدوات."},{"q":"","options":["The report explains what the problem is, why it matters and how it can be solved.","The report explains what the problem is, why it matters and the solution.","The report explains the problem, why it matters and how to solving it.","The report explains what the problem is, its importance and how solved."],"answer":"The report explains what the problem is, why it matters and how it can be solved.","explanation":"Parallel clauses.","explanation_ar":"الجمل المتوازية."},{"q":"Which bulleted list is parallel?","options":["Research the motion • Prepare arguments • Practice the speech","Research the motion • Preparing arguments • The speech practice","To research • Preparing • Practice","Researching • Prepare arguments • Speech"],"answer":"Research the motion • Prepare arguments • Practice the speech","explanation":"Parallel bulleted lists.","explanation_ar":"القوائم النقطية."},{"q":"","options":["The winners were articulate, preparation and respectful.","The winners were articulate, well prepared and showed respect toward their opponents.","The winners were articulate, well prepared and respectful toward their opponents.","The winners were articulation, well prepared and respectful."],"answer":"The winners were articulate, well prepared and respectful toward their opponents.","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"Many teachers believe that learning to debate is as valuable as ______ a second language, since both skills help students communicate with people who think differently.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["to learn","learning","learn","the learning of"],"answer":"learning","explanation":"Parallel comparisons.","explanation_ar":"المقارنة المتوازية."},{"q":"The tournament's organizers hope to attract more schools, to train new adjudicators and ______ the competition to other cities in the region.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the expansion of","expanding","they will expand","to expand"],"answer":"to expand","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"The adjudicators evaluated each team on the strength of its evidence, the clarity of its structure and ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["rebutting well","how well it rebutted","if its rebuttals were good","the quality of its rebuttals"],"answer":"the quality of its rebuttals","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"In a long series, where is a parallelism error most often hidden?","options":["in the verb before the list","in the last item","in the punctuation only","in the first item"],"answer":"in the last item","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Rhetoric and Debate” that shows parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Rhetoric and Debate” that shows parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences in action"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spelling Relay”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spelling Relay"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “To Organize, to Anticipate and to Respond: Parallelism in Complex Series”. Let’s learn parallel structure in long and complex series: the same grammatical form for every item (nouns, -ing forms, to-infinitives, clauses); repeating articles, prepositions or to for clarity (to organize, to anticipate and to respond); parallel comparisons (than / as); parallel elements with correlatives and in bulleted or numbered lists; SAT questions on faulty parallelism in long sentences together. In a series (a list of three or more items), every item must have the same grammatical form. ✔ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. ✔ organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly. ✘ (not: to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response). Long series often hide the error at the end: check the last item against the first. In a series (a list of three or more items), every item must have the same grammatical form. ✔ Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. ✔ organizing ideas, anticipating objections and responding calmly. ✘ (not: to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response). Long series often hide the error at the end: check the last item against the first. For clarity in long series, you can repeat the article, preposition or to before each item: in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life; to organize…, to anticipate… and to respond… Once you start repeating, continue for every item. Clauses in a series must also match: what speakers say, how they say it and why they say it. Here are some examples. Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly. Good speakers are clear, confident and respectful. The skills are useful in the classroom, in the debating hall and in public life. Be careful! Some learners say: “Debate teaches students to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response.” That’s not right. We say: “Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.” Same form. Quick check: \"to organize ideas, anticipating objections and a calm response\" – what's wrong? … Think! … The three items have different forms. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Debate teaches students to organize ideas, to anticipate objections and to respond calmly.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5","title":"This House Believes…: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to take part in a formal debate as a proposition or opposition speaker.","Students will be able to write a debate speech with correlatives, concession and parallel series, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can deliver a structured debate speech.","I can write a persuasive debate speech with a rebuttal."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من المشاركة في مناظرة رسمية متحدثين باسم المؤيدين أو المعارضين.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة كلمة مناظرة بأدوات الربط المزدوجة والتنازل والسلاسل المتوازية، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن ألقي كلمة مناظرة منظمة.","أستطيع أن أكتب كلمة مناظرة إقناعية مع رد على المعارضين."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"motion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmoʊʃən/","definition":"a formal proposal that is debated and voted on","arabic":"مقترح للنقاش / قضية المناظرة","example":"The motion was \"This House would ban homework.\"","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"contend","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kənˈtɛnd/","definition":"to argue or claim that something is true","arabic":"يحتجّ / يؤكّد","example":"The opposition contends that the plan is too expensive.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"substantiate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/səbˈstæntʃiˌeɪt/","definition":"to provide evidence that proves a claim","arabic":"يُثبت بالدليل","example":"She substantiated her claim with official statistics.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cogent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈkoʊdʒənt/","definition":"clear, logical and convincing","arabic":"مقنع ومحكم","example":"He presented a cogent argument for reform.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"undermine","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈʌndɚˌmaɪn/","definition":"to gradually weaken something","arabic":"يقوّض / يُضعف","example":"One false statistic can undermine an entire argument.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"bolster","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈboʊlstɚ/","definition":"to support or strengthen something","arabic":"يدعم / يعزّز","example":"New evidence bolstered their case.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"articulate","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ɑrˈtɪkjəˌleɪt/","definition":"able to express ideas clearly in words","arabic":"فصيح / واضح التعبير","example":"She is an articulate speaker.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"impartial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪmˈpɑrʃəl/","definition":"not supporting either side; fair","arabic":"محايد / نزيه","example":"The judges must be impartial.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"adjudicator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈdʒudɪˌkeɪtɚ/","definition":"a person who judges a competition or a dispute","arabic":"حَكَم","example":"The adjudicators announced the winning team.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"discredit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈskrɛdət/","definition":"to make people stop trusting someone or something","arabic":"يُفقد المصداقية / يشكّك في","example":"The lawyer tried to discredit the witness.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"reiterate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈɪtɚˌeɪt/","definition":"to repeat something to emphasize it","arabic":"يكرّر / يؤكّد مجددًا","example":"Let me reiterate my main point.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tenuous","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈtɛnjəwəs/","definition":"weak and not convincing","arabic":"واهٍ / ضعيف","example":"The link between the two events is tenuous.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"nuance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnuɑns/","definition":"a small but important difference in meaning or opinion","arabic":"فارق دقيق","example":"A good debater understands the nuances of an issue.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sweeping","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈswipɪŋ/","definition":"too general, without considering details or exceptions","arabic":"شامل وعام بلا تمييز","example":"\"All teenagers are lazy\" is a sweeping generalization.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"preempt","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈpriˌɛmpt/","definition":"to deal with something before it happens","arabic":"يستبق","example":"She preempted the opposition's argument in her opening speech.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 5: correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, and parallelism in complex series.\nA debate speech addresses the chair and audience, states the motion and your side, gives two or three arguments with evidence, rebuts or preempts the other side's case, and ends with a clear call to vote. Concessive structures show that you respect the opposition; parallel series and correlatives make your points memorable.\n✔ not only… but also • neither… nor • both… and\n✔ Much as I respect… • Hard as it is… • For all these reasons…\n✔ to work…, to understand… and to take… (parallel series)\n✔ motion, contend, contention, tenuous, cogent, substantiate, preempt","examples":["Community service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.","It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others and to take responsibility.","Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak.","The opposition contends that service should be voluntary.","This contention is tenuous.","Hard as it is to find time, volunteering is worth it.","Neither grades nor test scores can measure empathy.","For all these reasons, I urge you to support the motion."],"common_errors":["✗ It benefits not only the people who receive help but also students giving it. → ✓ It benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it. (Parallel clauses.)","✗ As much as I respect the opposition, but their argument is weak. → ✓ Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak. (No but.)","✗ It teaches teamwork, to understand others and taking responsibility. → ✓ It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others and to take responsibility. (Parallel series.)"],"arabic":"نراجع أدوات الربط المزدوجة والتوازي، وتراكيب التنازل الرفيعة، ومفردات المناظرة وعائلاتها، والتوازي في السلاسل المعقدة.\nكلمة المناظرة تخاطب رئيس الجلسة والجمهور، وتعرض القضية وموقفك، وتقدّم حجتين أو ثلاثًا بأدلة، وترد على حجج الطرف الآخر أو تستبقها، وتنتهي بدعوة للتصويت.\nتراكيب التنازل تُظهر احترامك للمعارضين، والسلاسل المتوازية وأدوات الربط المزدوجة تجعل نقاطك سهلة التذكر."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “This House Believes…: Language in Use”. Let’s learn correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U5-LS5 | This House Believes…: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Formal debate: in teams of three, debate the motion \"This House would make community service compulsory for all secondary students.\" Each speaker uses one correlative structure, one advanced concession (Much as…, Hard as it is…, For all…), one parallel series of three, two debate words, and one rebuttal that preempts or answers an opponent's argument.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 5: correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, and parallelism in complex series. A debate speech addresses the chair and audience, states the motion and your side, gives two or three arguments with evidence, rebuts or preempts the other side's case, and ends with a clear call to vote. Concessive structures show that you respect the opposition; parallel series and correlatives make your points memorable.","on_screen":"✔ not only… but also • neither… nor • both… and • ✔ Much as I respect… • Hard as it is… • For all these reasons… • ✔ to work…, to understand… and to take… (parallel series)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Community service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it. It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others and to take responsibility. Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak.","on_screen":"Community service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it. / It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others and to take responsibility. / Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “It benefits not only the people who receive help but also students giving it.” That’s not right. We say: “It benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.” Parallel clauses.","on_screen":"✗ It benefits not only the people who receive help but also students giving it.   ✓ It benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “As much as I respect the opposition, but their argument is weak.”? … Think! … It should be “Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can deliver a structured debate speech.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can deliver a structured debate speech.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع أدوات الربط المزدوجة والتوازي، وتراكيب التنازل الرفيعة، ومفردات المناظرة وعائلاتها، والتوازي في السلاسل المعقدة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: It benefits not only the people who receive help but also students giving it. — الصحيح: It benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن ألقي كلمة مناظرة منظمة."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura","body":"Madam Chair, adjudicators, ladies and gentlemen: this House believes that community service should be compulsory for all secondary students.\nCommunity service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.\nIt teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others' needs and to take responsibility for their communities.\nMuch as I respect the opposition's concern for students' free time, two hours a week is a small price to pay.\nThe opposition contends that compulsory service is no longer truly voluntary; however, this contention is tenuous.\nHard as it is to find time, students who volunteer often discover skills they never knew they had.\nNeither good grades nor test scores can measure the empathy that service develops.\nFor all these reasons, I urge you to support the motion.","word_count":134,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Noura's speech. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Noura speaks for the motion.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"She believes community service benefits only the people who receive help.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"She lists three things that community service teaches.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Noura completely ignores the opposition's concerns.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"She calls the opposition's contention tenuous.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Noura says that grades can measure empathy.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “For the Motion: Compulsory Community Service – Noura”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Community service benefits not only the people who receive help ___ the students who give it.","options":["but","and also","as well as","but also"],"answer":"but also","explanation":"Correlative conjunction or parallel series.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الربط والتوازي."},{"q":"It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others' needs and ___ responsibility.","options":["taking","they take","took","to take"],"answer":"to take","explanation":"Correlative conjunction or parallel series.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الربط والتوازي."},{"q":"___ I respect the opposition's concern, two hours a week is a small price to pay.","options":["Much as","Much","So much as","As much"],"answer":"Much as","explanation":"Advanced concessive structure.","explanation_ar":"التنازل."},{"q":"The opposition ___ that compulsory service is no longer voluntary.","options":["contending","contention","contends","contentious"],"answer":"contends","explanation":"Debate vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المناظرة."},{"q":"___ good grades nor test scores can measure empathy.","options":["Neither","Either","Both","Not only"],"answer":"Neither","explanation":"Correlative conjunction or parallel series.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الربط والتوازي."},{"q":"The opposition argues that students are already too busy to volunteer. ______ surveys show that most students spend several hours a week on social media.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","However,","Similarly,","For example,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your debate speech.","items":[{"q":"Address the chair and state your side: ___","options":[],"answer":"Madam Chair, ladies and gentlemen: I stand in opposition to the motion.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A correlative structure: ___","options":[],"answer":"Compulsory service would be neither fair to working students nor truly meaningful.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"An advanced concession: ___","options":[],"answer":"Hard as it is to argue against helping others, forcing students to volunteer misses the point.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A parallel series of three: ___","options":[],"answer":"Students already need time to study, to rest and to support their families.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A rebuttal with a debate word: ___","options":[],"answer":"The proposition's claim that service builds empathy is unsubstantiated; empathy cannot be forced.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your debate speech.","items":[{"q":"Plan: address, motion and side, two arguments, concession, rebuttal, call to vote.","options":[],"answer":"Against: compulsory service; arguments: not voluntary, students' time; concession: service is valuable; rebuttal: empathy can't be forced; call: oppose","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your debate speech for the side you chose (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"Madam Chair, adjudicators, ladies and gentlemen: we oppose the motion that community service should be compulsory. Much as we admire volunteers, service that is forced is neither voluntary nor meaningful. Students already need time to study, to rest and to support their families. The proposition contends that compulsory service builds empathy; however, this claim remains unsubstantiated. Hard as it is to measure empathy, research suggests that it grows from genuine choice, not from obligation. Moreover, compulsory programs would require both large budgets and many supervisors. For all its good intentions, the motion would turn kindness into homework. For these reasons, we urge you to oppose the motion.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: correlatives parallel? concessive structures without \"but\"? the last item of every series matches the first? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"to study, to rest and supporting their families\" to \"to study, to rest and to support their families\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Board Race Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team stands at the board.","The teacher says a cue word and a task (meaning, spelling or sentence with correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review)).","The first correct answer wins a point; rotate players every round.","Play 10 rounds."],"data":[{"cue":"motion","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"contend","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"substantiate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"cogent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"undermine","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"bolster","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"articulate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"impartial","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"adjudicator","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"discredit","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"reiterate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"tenuous","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"nuance","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"sweeping","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"preempt","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Either the proposition or the opposition ___ the final rebuttal.","options":["are giving","gives","give","have given"],"answer":"gives","explanation":"Correlative conjunction or parallel series.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الربط والتوازي."},{"q":"___ though it may sound, losing the debate was the best thing for our team.","options":["Stranger","Strangely","Strangeness","Strange"],"answer":"Strange","explanation":"Advanced concessive structure.","explanation_ar":"التنازل."},{"q":"The adjudicators praised her ___ argument; every point was logical and well supported.","options":["cogent","sweeping","tenuous","contentious"],"answer":"cogent","explanation":"Debate vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المناظرة."},{"q":"She ___ the opposition's main objection before they could raise it.","options":["discredited","reiterated","preempted","adjudicated"],"answer":"preempted","explanation":"Debate vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المناظرة."},{"q":"","options":["Debating improves students' confidence, logic and how they work in teams.","Debating improves students' confidence, logic and teamwork.","Debating improves students' confidence, logical and teamwork.","Debating improves students' confident, logic and teamwork."],"answer":"Debating improves students' confidence, logic and teamwork.","explanation":"Correlative conjunction or parallel series.","explanation_ar":"أدوات الربط والتوازي."},{"q":"The proposition presented strong statistics. ______ the opposition questioned whether the data were up to date.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Therefore,","As a result,","Similarly,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Both teams prepared carefully for months. ______ the final debate was one of the closest in the tournament's history.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Instead,","Nevertheless,","As a result,","In contrast,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• The Oxford Union is a debating society in Britain.\n• It was founded in 1823.\n• It has hosted debates on both serious and light-hearted topics.\n• Many famous politicians spoke there as students.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize the long history of the Oxford Union. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Founded in 1823, the Oxford Union has hosted debates for two centuries.","The Oxford Union is in Britain.","It hosts light-hearted debates.","Many politicians spoke there."],"answer":"Founded in 1823, the Oxford Union has hosted debates for two centuries.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Munazara is a traditional form of debate in Arabic culture.\n• Scholars used it to examine questions of law, language and philosophy.\n• Judges evaluated the quality of the reasoning, not only the conclusion.\n• Some universities have revived munazara competitions.\n\nThe student wants to explain what made munazara distinctive. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Arabic culture is rich.","Some universities have revived munazara.","Scholars examined questions of law.","In munazara, a traditional form of Arabic debate, judges evaluated not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning."],"answer":"In munazara, a traditional form of Arabic debate, judges evaluated not only the conclusion but also the quality of the reasoning.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best concluding sentence for a debate speech:","options":["As much as all these reasons, support it.","For all these reasons, but I urge you to support the motion.","For all these reasons, I urge you to support the motion.","Neither these reasons or the motion, please vote."],"answer":"For all these reasons, I urge you to support the motion.","explanation":"Advanced concessive structure.","explanation_ar":"التنازل."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A formal school debate: the motion \"This House would make community service compulsory for all secondary students\" on a screen; Noura speaking for the proposition; Saad taking notes for the opposition; three adjudicators at a table. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A formal school debate: the motion \"This House would make community service compulsory for all secondary students\" on a screen; Noura speaking for the proposition; Saad taking notes for the opposition; three adjudicators at a table"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Rhetoric and Debate”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Rhetoric and Debate”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L12-U5-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Board Race Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Board Race Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS5-T1","script":"Madam Chair, adjudicators, ladies and gentlemen: this House believes that community service should be compulsory for all secondary students. Community service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it. It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others' needs and to take responsibility for their communities. Much as I respect the opposition's concern for students' free time, two hours a week is a small price to pay. The opposition contends that compulsory service is no longer truly voluntary; however, this contention is tenuous. Hard as it is to find time, students who volunteer often discover skills they never knew they had. Neither good grades nor test scores can measure the empathy that service develops. For all these reasons, I urge you to support the motion.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U5-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “This House Believes…: Language in Use”. Let’s learn correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, parallelism in complex series; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Formal debate: in teams of three, debate the motion \"This House would make community service compulsory for all secondary students.\" Each speaker uses one correlative structure, one advanced concession (Much as…, Hard as it is…, For all…), one parallel series of three, two debate words, and one rebuttal that preempts or answers an opponent's argument. Today you use all of Unit 5: correlative conjunctions and parallelism, advanced concessive clauses, debate vocabulary and word families, and parallelism in complex series. A debate speech addresses the chair and audience, states the motion and your side, gives two or three arguments with evidence, rebuts or preempts the other side's case, and ends with a clear call to vote. Concessive structures show that you respect the opposition; parallel series and correlatives make your points memorable. Here are some examples. Community service benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it. It teaches young people to work in teams, to understand others and to take responsibility. Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak. Be careful! Some learners say: “It benefits not only the people who receive help but also students giving it.” That’s not right. We say: “It benefits not only the people who receive help but also the students who give it.” Parallel clauses. Quick check: what is wrong with “As much as I respect the opposition, but their argument is weak.”? … Think! … It should be “Much as I respect the opposition, their argument is weak.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can deliver a structured debate speech.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The training improved not only our speaking ___ our listening.","options":["but","and also","as well","but also"],"answer":"but also","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"Neither the captain nor the other speakers ___ ready.","options":["has been","is","were","was"],"answer":"were","explanation":"Agreement with either / neither… or / nor.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"___ she prepare the opening speech, but she also wrote the rebuttal.","options":["Not only did","Not only","Only not did","Not only she"],"answer":"Not only did","explanation":"Not only… but also (with inversion).","explanation_ar":"Not only."},{"q":"She writes not only speeches ___ articles for the school magazine.","options":["and also","but also","as well","but she also writes"],"answer":"but also","explanation":"Placement of correlative parts.","explanation_ar":"موضع أداة الربط."},{"q":"The best debaters win with reasoning rather than ___.","options":["loud","with volume","to shout","shouting at"],"answer":"with volume","explanation":"rather than / whether… or with parallel forms.","explanation_ar":"rather than."},{"q":"The new program aims to help students both to express their ideas clearly ______ to respect opinions that differ from their own.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["or","but also","nor","and"],"answer":"and","explanation":"Parallel elements after correlatives.","explanation_ar":"التوازي."},{"q":"___ I admire his confidence, his evidence was weak.","options":["Much","As much","Much as","So much as"],"answer":"Much as","explanation":"Much as + clause.","explanation_ar":"Much as."},{"q":"___ as it is to admit, our opponents were better prepared.","options":["Hardness","Harder","Hardly","Hard"],"answer":"Hard","explanation":"Adjective / adverb + as / though + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"Hard as it is."},{"q":"___, I couldn't find a flaw in her argument.","options":["Try as might I","Try as I might","As I try might","Trying as I might"],"answer":"Try as I might","explanation":"However + adjective; Try as I might.","explanation_ar":"Try as I might."},{"q":"The debate was a success, ___ a short one.","options":["despite","for all","albeit","although it"],"answer":"albeit","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"______ the heavy rain that delayed several teams, the final of the national debating championship began only twenty minutes late.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Although","Even though","Albeit","Despite"],"answer":"Despite","explanation":"despite / notwithstanding grammar.","explanation_ar":"despite."},{"q":"She ___ her claim with data from three reliable studies.","options":["reiterated","substantiated","adjudicated","undermined"],"answer":"substantiated","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"\"Everyone who plays video games is violent\" is a ___ generalization.","options":["tenuous","sweeping","cogent","nuanced"],"answer":"sweeping","explanation":"Debate collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The link between the two events is ___; the evidence is weak.","options":["tenuous","substantial","cogent","articulate"],"answer":"tenuous","explanation":"Debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Although the opposition's speakers were confident, their central claim remained ______: they offered no data or examples to support it.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["substantive","cogent","unsubstantiated","substantial"],"answer":"unsubstantiated","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The topic is ___; people hold strong opinions on both sides.","options":["contending","contention","contentious","contend"],"answer":"contentious","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"","options":["Debate teaches students to research, to argue and to listen.","Debate teaches students to research, to argue and listening.","Debate teaches students to research, arguing and listening to.","Debate teaches students researching, to argue and to listen."],"answer":"Debate teaches students to research, to argue and to listen.","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"","options":["Thinking carefully is as important as speaking well.","Thinking carefully is as important as to speak well.","To think carefully is as important as speaking well.","Thinking carefully is as important as your speech is good."],"answer":"Thinking carefully is as important as speaking well.","explanation":"Parallel comparisons.","explanation_ar":"المقارنة المتوازية."},{"q":"The club hopes to recruit new members, to organize a regional tournament and ______ a series of public debates on local issues.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["it will host","to host","hosting","the hosting of"],"answer":"to host","explanation":"Parallel items in a series.","explanation_ar":"التوازي في السلسلة."},{"q":"The proposition argued that homework improves discipline. ______ the opposition presented evidence that excessive homework increases stress without improving results.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","As a result,","Similarly,","In response,"],"answer":"In response,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• In competitive debate, sides are often assigned at random.\n• Debaters may have to argue for a view they disagree with.\n• This can be difficult.\n• It helps them understand the strongest arguments on both sides.\n\nThe student wants to explain a benefit of random side assignment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Although arguing for a view they disagree with can be difficult, debaters assigned sides at random learn to understand the strongest arguments on both sides.","Debate can be difficult.","Sides are often assigned at random.","Debaters disagree with views."],"answer":"Although arguing for a view they disagree with can be difficult, debaters assigned sides at random learn to understand the strongest arguments on both sides.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"as much as i respect her, but she not only spoke too fast but her evidence was weak.\"","options":["Much as I respect her, she not only spoke too fast but also offered weak evidence.","As much as I respect her, she not only spoke too fast but also offered weak evidence.","much as I respect her, she not only spoke too fast but also offered weak evidence.","Much as I respect her, but she not only spoke too fast but also offered weak evidence."],"answer":"Much as I respect her, she not only spoke too fast but also offered weak evidence.","explanation":"Much as + clause.","explanation_ar":"Much as."},{"q":"What does \"albeit\" mean in \"The plan worked, albeit slowly\"?","options":["therefore","unless","although","because"],"answer":"although","explanation":"For all + noun; albeit.","explanation_ar":"For all / albeit."},{"q":"Which sentence uses \"even if\" correctly?","options":["Even if she was tired last night, she prepared.","Even if it rained yesterday, the debate went ahead.","Even if it is raining now, so we stayed home.","Even if it rains tomorrow, the debate will go ahead."],"answer":"Even if it rains tomorrow, the debate will go ahead.","explanation":"even though vs even if.","explanation_ar":"even though أم even if."}],"theme_ar":"البلاغة والمناظرة","essential_question_ar":"هل الفوز في الجدال يعني أنك على حق؟","can_do":"I can use correlative conjunctions with precise parallel structure and agreement, express concession with sophisticated structures (much as, hard as it is, for all, albeit), use the vocabulary of debate and its word families, and keep long, complex series parallel.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم أدوات الربط المزدوجة مع توازٍ دقيق في البنية واتفاق صحيح، وأعبّر عن التنازل بتراكيب رفيعة (much as / hard as it is / for all / albeit)، وأستخدم مفردات المناظرة وعائلات كلماتها، وأحافظ على التوازي في السلاسل الطويلة المعقدة."});
